## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) turned out as a new class of materials when \(\mathrm{Cu}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bpy})_{2.5}\mathrm{NO}_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{1.25}\) was described as a MOF by Yaghi's group in 1995 [1]. As illustrated in Fig. 1, MOFs are considered as three-dimensionally connected networks comprising of metal nodes that are linked by organic bridging molecules.
Since the prototype of MOF-5 with chemical composition of \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}\) [where \(\mathrm{BDC}=1,4\)-benzenedicarboxylate] was reported [2], numerous research efforts have been directed towards the discovery of new types of MOFs, and to date, over 20,000 different kinds of MOFs have been synthesized [3]. The structural properties of several typical MOFs such as MOF-5 [4], UiO-66 [3], HKUST-1 [5], and MIL-101 [6] have been studied extensively, as shown in Fig. 2. Owing to the fact that MOFs are highly ordered solids, theoretically most MOFs are often reported to have perfect shapes and structures. For example, MOF-5 crystals in Fig. 2a show the cubic three-dimensional structure with \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\) nodes at each corner of a cube coordinated by six terephthalate ions, which make them look beautiful and perfect.
Most of the initial excitement on MOF materials was mainly focused on the classical applications of MOFs as microporous materials for storage of gases such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Interests in gas storage topics were largely derived from MOFs typical textural properties such as high surface areas and pore volumes, which are critical factors that lead to the increased van der Waals interaction that is needed for enhanced uptake capacities of gases. Typically, MOFs are synthesized in solutions containing metal cations and electron donors. The presence of different metal centres and acid linkers enables the pore size, hydro- and/or thermal stabilities to be tailored to achieve desired properties. For example, MOF-5 was found to decompose easily in air, which would certainly hinder its further real-world practical applications. The later discovered UiO-66 (Zr) and HKUST-1 (Cu) MOFs types showed good hydro- and/or thermal stabilities. To realize green synthesis by substituting the environmentally unfriendly solvent dimethylformamide (DMF), MIL-101(Cr)-type MOF synthesized using water as a solvent, was discovered by F´erey's group [6]. With such developments of MOF materials, the newly-discovered properties have extended their potential range of applications to luminescent resonance imaging, drug delivery, electronic materials, and others [7–9].
