Several studies have reported that structure of the experiment-derived "real crystals" at times digresses from that of a perfect arrangement and often has a substantial degree of structural irregularities or compositional inhomogeneities. For example, as demonstrated in Fig. 3 [10], the experiment-derived HKUST-1 crystal is apparently perfect with well-defined octahedral shape, however, the characterization results indicated that the HKUST-1 crystals are not as structurally perfect as they seem to be. This will further be explained in Section 2.
It is common to use the concepts of heterogeneity and structural disorders of different nature interchangeably with the term
"structural defects". Ideally, it is advantageous to have defect-free crystals for some specific applications such as optoelectronics [11]. Nevertheless, the structural defects or disorders from many other key classes of materials enable the expression of important properties and functions, such as semi-conduction in silicon-related or Group III/V compounds [12], spin frustration in cooperative paramagnets [13], induce thermoelectric behaviour [14] and encourage the formation of polar nanodomain in relaxor ferroelectrics [15]. Similarly, certain degree of structural defects is promising to offer other positive effects in the physical and chemical properties of MOF materials.
Several research groups have reviewed and defined 'structural defects' in relation to structural disorders and heterogeneities that interrupt the periodic arrangement of atoms [16–18]. For example, Cliffe et al. [17] defined MOF structural defects as 'indications of non-stoichiometry' in the MOF structure. Furukawa et al. [19] also extends this definition to include other kinds of imperfections such as presence of vacancies caused by missing linkers as well as the availability of multiple pore sizes, among others. Wu et al. [18] further reported that MOF defects can either occur intrinsically or be introduced intentionally. Fang and her co-workers [20] gave a comprehensive review of defective coordination network
compounds (CNCs) and highlighted the importance of "defect-engineering" concepts. As outlined, on condition that no other changes are performed during the synthesis process apart from the mixing of the typical building blocks of the parent framework under conventional synthetic conditions, the resulting MOFs are reported to be susceptible to exhibiting "inherent defects" that emanate either from misconnections or dislocations happening during the crystallization stage [21] or from post-crystallization cleavage [22,23]. Influenced by the different synthetic conditions, inherent defects have been found in several MOFs such as MOF-5 [24], UiO-66 [25], HKUST-1 [26], and MIL-101 [27]. Very recently, Sholl and Lively [28] published another overview of the current
understanding of defects in MOFs. In this case they used the terminology MOFs defects in its "broadest possible sense" by referring to MOF defects as "any deviations from an ideally ordered crystal structure". The insight from zeolite research community was borrowed to draw the analogies between defects in MOFs and well-known defective structures in zeolites. They highlighted that "work on describing and controlling defects in MOFs is at a nascent stage" and "the likely ubiquitous nature of defects in MOFs implies that efforts to systematically understand them will yield considerable benefits in controlling material properties". Earlier works indicated that even the most carefully synthesized MOFs must contain a variety of structural defects and those defects may endow specific properties to the application that is envisioned for the materials of choice. In fact, there have been rapidly growing investigations concerning structural defects in MOFs towards various applications since 2008 (Fig. 4).
Although structural defects in MOF materials have been mentioned in numerous earlier reports [29–32], detailed knowledge of the mechanisms controlling the formation, nature and concentration of defects need to be available to aid the proper use of this phenomenon in a positive way. In this review article, we highlight and summarize recent developments that concern the formation, detection and control of MOFs' defects towards applications of interests.
## 2. Formation of structural defects in MOFs
An easy understanding of MOFs' defects suggesting that defects are only randomly formed especially during the synthesis of MOFs has been put forward. However, studies by Cliffe et al. [17] indicated that MOFs' defects can actually be introduced and even
controlled. This claim was demonstrated using a hafnium terephthalate MOF (Fig. 5). There are several studies that have reported that nanoscale defect structures have implications for MOFs properties and hence influencing their application [34,35].
As introduced in Section 1, a number of ways have been demonstrated from literature to introduce defects into MOF structures (Fig. 6) [19,34–37]. These strategies can be narrowed down to two primary routes namely: (a) during synthesis of MOFs and (b) during post-synthetic modifications of MOFs. Apart from the mixing of organic linkers, metal centres or other functional groups during the synthesis, the modulation synthesis and some postsynthetic modifications will be emphasized here.
## 2.1. Modulation synthesis of MOFs
In many cases, the synthesis of crystalline MOFs cannot be straightforward, since many attempts of combining metal ions with organic linkers often result in amorphous products [38]. In 2009, Kitagawa group [39,40] dealt with the problem of fabricating MOF nanoparticles with certain sizes, shapes and higher crystallinity in comparison to the less crystalline bulk materials obtained from conventional solvothermal synthesis. The addition of a modulator in the synthesis solution was understood to lead to the modulating molecules coordination with the metal ion without acting as bridging molecule and thus "modulating" the three-dimensional expansion of the framework. To date, modulation synthesis has been widely used as an effective tool for increasing the reproducibility of the synthesis process, improving crystallinity of the MOF products and also facilitating the control of material properties [41]. However, there are several different models from
the scattered literature to understand the role played by modulator, and sometimes they may conflict with each other. We summarize them into four models:
## 2.1.1. Model I: modulator as capping agent
In some conditions, modulator acts as capping agent to slow down the reactions and control the shapes and sizes of the MOF crystals [37]. As illustrated in Fig. 7, without adding any modulator, the growth of a MOF framework is understood to occur very fast. As a consequence, defects or disorders can form in the crystals within small and intergrown particles. While the modulator decreases the nucleation rate, it also leads to highly crystalline, non-aggregated crystals as the modulating molecules act as capping agents so that the intergrowth of nuclei is inhibited. Obviously, the application of a modulation synthesis allows the control of the particle sizes, shapes and also enables reproducibility of the synthesis process [43,44].
## 2.1.2. Model II: modulator as deprotonating agent
Apart from the coordination modulation mechanism, a modulator (during a modulation synthesis) can also act as a deprotonating agent to facilitate the deprotonation of the targeted linking molecule [45]. As a result, the presence of modulator will accelerate the reaction rate and influence the crystallization behaviour. In the DMF-based reaction systems, water is somehow essential for the hydrolysis of the metal precursor and also for the oxygen supply required for the formation of secondary-building units (SBUs). DMF easily absorbs water and decomposes as shown in Eq. (1) [46,47]:
In the case of using formic acid as the modulator, the above equilibrium could be displaced to the left side to make more water available in the reaction system for the hydrolysis of the metal precursor. By this indirect means, formic acid firstly speeds up the formation of the SBUs and then accelerates the formation of the nuclei, and in the end the available excess SBUs allows the nuclei to grow into larger crystals. This agrees well with the visual observation that the addition of formic acid to the synthesis introduced the quicker formation of the seeding crystals and the higher equivalent of formic acid gave the larger sizes of MOF crystals [48].
## 2.1.3. Model III: modulator as reacting reagent
In general, acid-modulated reaction might commence with a fast formation of the soluble discrete modulator-capped metal clusters, followed by their assembly into the ultimate 3D structure through reversible ligand exchange with the ditopic linker acid [49]. In such circumstance, the applied modulator participates in the reaction and becomes part of the derived MOF structure, resulting in the formation of structural defects. In some cases, however, these defect-inducing modulators can be removed postsynthesis via thermal activation to expose open metal sites that are Lewis acids, as demonstrated by Jiang and colleagues [50] on
a defect-containing sulphone-functionalized MOF (USTC-253-TFA). Furthermore, rapid crystallization times are reported to likely lead to MOF products with incomplete assembly that are intrinsically more defective than MOF crystals synthesized from slower reaction [51].
2.1.4. Model IV: functionalized-modulator as the secondary reagent
Interestingly, Shearer et al. [52] envisioned that functionalizedmodulators could be incorporated into MOF structures by compensating for missing cluster defects. These introduced functional groups would provide additional sites for chemical interaction and would certainly provide new opportunities for MOF research community. In this case the MOF chemistry would no longer be restricted to certain linker derivatives in order to introduce organic functionalities to MOFs.
## 2.1.5. Factors influencing the reaction routes
Defects controlled in the modulated reactions are generally classified as 'control on concentration of defects' and 'control on arrangement of defects' [21,29]. The important influencing factors recognized in literature are; difference in solvent systems, nature of the modulators of choice, amount of modulators and nature of metal centres. These factors are discussed below.
## (a) Solvent-dependent roles
The solvent of choice in the modulated reactions will have an impact on the kinetics of the MOF crystallization. For example, the in-situ synchrotron diffraction studies from Behrens group [53] showed that the addition of formic acid
as a modulator slowed down the formation of Zr-fum MOF in the \(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\)-based synthesis, while in the DMF-based synthesis; the addition of formic acid accelerated the formation of Zr-fum MOF. This unexpected solvent-based effect was attributed to the availability of small amount of water that was present in the formic acid. The schematic illustration of the principle of the synchrotron radiation and results are shown in Fig. 8.
(b) Nature of the modulator of choice
The modulators of greater acidity tend to introduce higher concentration of defects, while the judicious choice of modulators has been found to determine the defects' phases [57–59]. Under the circumstance that the applied modulator participates in the formation of MOF structures, there is the possibility that some of the modulator remains in the
structure and causes the "missing linker defects" in the main structure [60]. As concluded by Gutov et al. [61], defects in Fig. 9 exhibited strong dependence on the nature of the modulator acid employed in the MOF synthesis, and hence presenting a promising unique set of MOF physical and chemical properties. Recently, studies by Cai et al. [62] reported on the synthesis of modulator-induced defective hierarchical MOFs (HP-MOFs) that possessed high stability and controllable pore characteristics. They further suggested that their synthetic strategy could be adapted not only to functionalized and elongated UiO structures but also to other varieties of MOFs.
Vermoortele et al. [63] demonstrated the use of synthesis modulation strategy as a valuable tool for increasing the chemical activity of Zr-MOF material by adding
trifluoroacetic acid during the synthesis process. Trifluoroacetate groups replaced part of the terephthalate linkers in the resulting materials and generated structural defects. Moreover, the thermal activation of the Zr-MOF material not only led to dihydroxylation of the hexanuclear Zr cluster but also resulted in the removal of the trifluoroacetate groups which led to a large number of open sites. Later, Vandichel et al. [64] constructed free energy diagrams that were used to rationalize the function of the modulators under real synthesis conditions. The application of theoretical rationalization process may lay the foundation for detailed studies on active site engineering. Yu et al. [65] developed a two-photon responsive MOF crystal ZJL-56-0.20 by a spatial modulation
of the fluorescence property of a MOF through the incorporation of a photoactive linker. The defective MOFs can be used for two-photon imaging, 3D patterning, as data storage media and further for sensor arrays.
(c) Amount of modulator
Li et al. [66] demonstrated that by changing the amount of modulator in the synthesis mixture, the structural defects in MOFs arising from the missing linkers could be systematically adjusted and hence enabling controllable decontamination of roxarsone from aqueous solution. The role of the modulator used in Zr-MOF synthesis is not only for modulating the crystal growth, but it also participates in the formation of the framework and promotes defects. Similarly, other
researchers [36,67] also reported on the effect of variation of modulator/linker ratio and highlighted that it has an impact of MOF crystal morphology, degree of crystallinity and also on the type and concentration of defects.
## (d) The nature of metal centres
Studies conducted by Diring et al. [39] indicated that in the case of the modulated synthesis of HKUST-1, the modulator that was used did not induce significant defects within the framework. This was explained by the unstable nature of
the missing-linker defects in MOFs with low network connections as is the case with HKUST-1. A recent study by Zhang et al. [68] reported on the impact of both solvent and metal ion source on the HKUST-1 defect chemistry. The study further suggested that the variation of solvents used, modification of the metal ion sources as well as the degree of doping levels of defect linkers are important parameters to be considered when studying defects in MOFs.
## 2.2. Post-synthetic modification of MOFs
Post-synthetic modifications are the processes whereby preformed MOFs undergo solid-solution reactions or surface modifications to incorporate more functionality. Firstly, post-synthetic modifications of parent MOFs could introduce diverse chemical functionalities into existing MOF structures instead of developing new conditions for MOF synthesis [69]. The solid-solution reactions may lead to mixed-component MOFs such as mixed-linker MOFs, mixed-metal MOFs or core–shell MOFs. The induced variations provide the potentials to tune the pore sizes, compositions and other properties. This topic has been reviewed by Burrows [70]. Secondly, post-synthetic treatments would allow a variety of active defects' sites to be generated in the MOF structures for the enhanced functionalities [71]. Wang et al. [72] reported the tuneable coordinative defects in UHM-3 surface-mounted MOFs that could be used for gas adsorption and separation. As shown in Fig. 10, an oriented grown, homogeneous and an almost defect-free (less than 1%) UHM-3 MOF thin films were obtained on a solid substrate using a liquid phase epitaxy method at room-temperature. Then, a thermal post-synthetic treatment was applied to induce \( \mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{I}) \) defect sites in a controlled manner.
Thirdly, the presence of defects would offer additional opportunities to conduct the secondary phase of post-synthetic modification on the existing MOF structures. For instance, certain surface modifications on the pre-formed MOFs would allow a better
loading of catalyst-like materials onto MOFs as composites [73–82]. Lastly, some studies demonstrated that the employment of the post-synthetic modification allowed the attainment of certain materials which cannot be obtained using other synthetic methods [83–85].
## 3. Detection of structural defects in MOF materials
Considering the impact of defects on physico-chemical properties of MOFs, the most straightforward consequence will be the increased cavities in the crystal structure and the decreased
density as well as the compromised thermal and mechanical properties of the material [86]. The first indication of the presence of structural defects or linker vacancies in MOFs came from poorer thermal stability of what was considered as "defect-free" MOF structure [87]. From literature, several techniques or their combinations have been employed to detect the structural defects in MOFs including powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), high-resolution neutron powder diffraction (HRNPD), Ultraviolet–Visible (UV–vis) spectroscopy, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), BET surface area \(\left(S_{\mathrm{BET}}\right)\) and pore volume \(\left(V_{\mathrm{pore}}\right)\) measurements, Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy, electrospray ionization-mass spectrometry (ESI-MS), nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SXRD), atomic force microscopy (AFM), confocal fluorescence microscopy (CFM), and the combination of different techniques.
## 3.1. PXRD: the presence of symmetry forbidden reflections
From the comparison of PXRD patterns of UiO-66 (Hf) and UiO-66 \((Z\mathbf{r})\) , Jakobsen et al. [88] observed two broad peaks of lower intensity appearing at 2θ lower than the (1 1 1) reflection \((7.4^{\circ})\) . They claimed that these two peaks represented forbidden reflections known for the topological space group (Fm-3m, 225). These peaks would disappear upon activation, indicating a solvent effect. Diffuse scatterings (Fig. 11) were observed by Øien et al. [89] at the expected positions of the symmetry-forbidden diffraction peaks of UiO-66, suggesting that there is some long-range order of the defects and/or missing clusters. This has been explained in detail by the earlier work [17,90]. In Fig. 12, two reflections that are symmetry forbidden were labelled by Shearer and co-workers [91].
## 3.2. HRNPD: existence of missing-linker defects
To identify the existence of the missing-linker defects in the MOF structures, Wu et al. [92] performed high-resolution neutron powder diffraction measurement on a deuterated UiO-66(Zr) sample synthesized from a 'modulator approach'. In comparison to PXRD, neutron diffraction is thought to be a better choice due to its equally sensitive nature to the organic linkers and the metal centres in the structure since the coherent neutron scatterings across sections of Zr, O, C and D are 6.44, 4.232, 5.551 and 5.592 barns, respectively, which are close to each other [92,93].
As a consequence, as shown in Fig. 13, neutron power diffraction measurement afforded a much better chance of 'seeing' the missing-linker defects when compared to use of X-ray diffraction technique. It should be noted that deuteration of the samples was recommended to substantially eliminate the incoherent scattering background that would else be produced by a regular hydrogenous sample, and thus allowing the attainment of high-quality diffraction patterns to follow the subtle structural information regarding linker vacancies.
## 3.3. UV–vis: DRIFTS spectra with vibrational modes
To understand the nature and types of defects in MOF structures, Szilágyi et al. [94] employed post-synthetic modification on MIL-101(Cr) and UV–vis spectroscopy to disclose the
information on metal-linker bonds, as shown in Fig. 14. The \(-\mathrm{OH}\) and \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) stretching modes revealed the presence of uncoordinated carboxylate groups. They also found the vibrational shifts to be proportional to the substitution of the functional groups, and deduced that the defects generated were dangling linkers.
## 3.4. TGA: less weight loss than theoretical expected
The unexpected observations from TGA measurements can give an indication of non-stoichiometric MOF structures and further supports the hypothesis that some of the linkers are missing from the frameworks. In this case, the observed weight loss during thermal decomposition is often significantly lower than the expected theoretical weight loss, indicating lighter frameworks compared to that formulated by the idealized equations [95–99].
As an example, our study on UiO-66 is highlighted here [100]. As shown in Fig. 15, the mass at \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was taken as the dry mass for all the UiO-66 samples, and it corresponds to the point after the initial mass losses attributed to dehydration and desolvation of the MOF materials [100]. Although both the physisorbed water and solvent molecules cannot be exactly defined stoichiometrically during TGA measurements, the loss of benzene ring can be distinctively defined [101]. As reported the plateau observed in the 300–650 \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) range of TGA plots indicates the chemical formula, \(\mathrm{ZrO}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})\) \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{4}\right)\), per Zr atom [100]. The mass difference that occurs in this range is attributed to the mass loss as a result of the decomposition
of the materials and the formation of \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}\), as above \(650\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) the mass stays constant for all samples. A theoretical mass loss of \(54.6\%\) for UiO-66 in relation to the mass at \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) should be reached at the end, taking into consideration the decomposition of the BDC linkers. As shown in Fig. 13, the relative weight losses for UiO-66 samples from 2, 4, 8 and \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) synthesis are 31.2, 32.8, 35.4 and \(39.4\%\), respectively, implying an increasing content of linkers per unit.
## 3.5. \(S_{BET}/V_{pore}\) measurements: higher values than theoretically expected
Given the defective MOF structures, the gas adsorption behaviours may deviate from those expected for defect-free crystals
[102,103]. As pointed out by Psofogiannakis et al. [104], the diffusion of a gas in MOF materials can be greatly assisted by structural defects. As known, the standard measurements of BET surface area and pore volume are calculated through \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) (or He) gas sorption isotherms on a per gram basis [105,106]. Given that MOF materials with missing linkers have a tendency to have lower sample densities, defective MOF samples will be found to exhibit higher surface area and pore volume if the percentage of missing linkers is larger [107,108]. For instance, the theoretical pore volume \((0.4\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and BET surface area \((954\,\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) are moderate for a defect-free UiO-66 crystal with composition, \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{6}\). If one linker was artificially removed for every metal centre in the unit cell, the linker deficiencies would include a porous framework, the calculated pore volume \((0.5\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and BET surface area \((1433\,\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) would be remarkably higher [109]. The existence of structural defects in MOFs can support the conclusion that the experimental BET values are higher than calculated values [110].
The simulated models of UiO-66, in which zero to six linkers are missing per unit cell (Fig. 16a), showed that the nitrogen uptake systematically increases as the number of linker deficiencies increases. Furthermore, a strong linear correlation between the number of missing linkers per unit cell and the surface area of the UiO-66 MOF material was observed (Fig. 16b).
## 3.6. FTIR: multiple O-H stretching bands
The occurrence of defects in MOF structures suggests the absence of negatively charged linkers. To maintain the charge neutrality of the MOF structure, during the modulation synthesis, the dangling bonds of the positively charged inorganic moieties have to be compensated by for example, \((OH)_{2}\) functional groups either from the modulator or solvent. As a result, more \(\mathrm{O-H}\) stretching bands can be expected and can consequently be detectable by FTIR. With such an assumption, FTIR has been employed to detect the multiple \(\mathrm{O-H}\) stretching bands [111-113].
In our early study [111], the in-situ IR technique (Mettler Toledo EasyMax 102 sets reactor coupled with a ReactIR 15 spectrometer) as shown in Fig. 17a was employed to monitor the crystallization process of Zr-fumarate (Zr-fum) MOF under real synthesis conditions. In Fig. 17b, the 3D plot of FTIR spectra collected over time showed the consumption of reactants and the appearance of the Zr-fum MOF product under DMF-based system, indicating the participations of both modulator acid (HCOOH) and
solvent (DMF) by compensating for missing linker or cluster defects during the structural formation of Zr-fum MOF. This observation will contribute to the quest for understanding of the formation of structural defects in MOFs.
3.7. NMR spectroscopy: sign of modulator elements or different linkers
Depending on the employed synthesis condition for producing MOF materials, a significant portion of the acid linkers are replaced by other species that originate either from the modulator or solvent. Since NMR spectroscopy is the most routinely applied technique for indicating the relative degrees of incorporation of linkers in a MOF structure, it has also been employed to do the element analysis and detect signs of additional functional groups derived from modulator or solvent elements [114–116]. For instance, Yaghi and co-workers [117] used \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) CP/MAS NMR spectra to show the incorporation of different dicarboxylates into MOF structures (Fig. 18).
## 3.8. ESI-MS: further modulator or linker information
Since NMR studies on a bulk material is unable to differentiate between a solid solution with two different mixed ligands at a molecular level and a mechanical mixed system of two compounds, ESI-MS technique which entails performing analyses on individual single crystals can be used to provide further ligand information for MOF structures [118–121].
## 3.9. SXRD: definitive molecular level detection of MOF defects
The involvement of different combined techniques to detect structural defects in MOFs has shed some light on the presence of such defects. However, the nature of those defects has not been fully determined yet. Yaghi group [122] took UiO-66 \(\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}\)
\(\left(\mathsf{C g}_{11}\mathsf{O}_{4}\right)_{6}\) as a representative example and interpreted the nature of defects from SXRD data. In their study, the effect of sample activation (in an environment cell) was employed to study the structure and the nature of the defects in-situ and in real-time under vacuum. From the results presented in Fig. 19 and Table 1, the linker defective sites were identified to be occupied by water, whereby the charge neutrality was maintained by hydroxide anions hydrogen bonding to the \(\upmu^{3}\) -OH groups of the metal cluster in the crystal structure of UiO-66. This conclusion was supported by Katz et al. [123].
## 3.10. AFM: reveal the crystal form, defects and MOF growth
Shöaèè et al. [124] proposed the use of high-resolution AFM study to reveal the features of the crystal growth and types of defects in MOFs such as HKUST-1 shown in Fig. 20. This can be used to reveal many previously unknown fundamental features of the crystal surface, growth and even structural defects in the MOFs, which is expected to have key implications in the tunability and uses of the crystalline MOFs for emerging applications.
## 3.11. CFM: three-dimensional visualization of defects
As indicated by Serre [125], it would be easier to establish the relationship between defect formation and MOF performance if the defects in MOFs could be visualized. The analytical techniques discussed have some shortcomings such as the inability to offer insight at every stage of defect formation or the fact that they reveal defects only at the exterior surfaces of MOF crystals. An excellent study by Ameloot et al. [126] demonstrated that CFM can be used as a powerful tool for enabling three-dimensional visualization of defects in single crystals of MOFs that are based on carboxylate linkers. The fluorescence signal observed in this study was generated by the catalytic oligomerization of furfuryl
alcohol that was used as the probe molecule, on acidic sites that resulted from the cleavage of metal-carboxylate bonds during the formation of defects [127]. The authors first demonstrated this method on HKUST-1 MOF single crystals, as shown in Fig. 21, indicating the introduction of defects upon prolonging crystallization time. They then tested on MOF-5 single crystals and the square-shaped defects on the outer surface were observed (Fig. 22). These types of features could be ascribed to the growth mechanism of the MOF-5 crystal faces.
## 3.12. The combination of different techniques
Valenzano and colleagues [128] disclosed the structure defectivity in UiO-66 sample through the combination of
temperature-resolved PXRD and TGA techniques, as shown in Fig. 23. The TGA instrument was also coupled with a quadrupole mass spectrometer (MS) for identification of the chemical nature of the species leaving the sample during the TGA experiment. The parallel TGA/MS experiment was performed to (i) confirm PXRD results, (ii) determine the range of temperature required to remove the solvent, and (iii) understand the chemical nature of the species/fragments formed upon collapse of the framework. Assuming that the final residue of UiO-66 sample at high temperature is \( \mathrm{ZrO}_{2} \) and a weight loss of \( 54.6\% \) is expected in the last step the lower weight loss of \( 36\% \) (from Fig. 23b) indicated a defective structure with missing linkers. MS data in Fig. 23c–e shows the sequence of water, DMF and benzene coming off the UiO-66 sample in the heating temperature range. Other research groups also
demonstrated the necessity of combination of different techniques to identify the structural defects in MOFs [129–132].
## 4. Defects' control towards practices of interests
Similar to other technologies that have transitioned from discovery to applications, after 20 years of their discovery, research on MOF materials will move to application-oriented direction in the next 50 years. To date, the potential for various industrial applications of MOF materials has attracted increasing interests [130]. For most applications, thermal and hydrothermal stabilities and sometimes chemical stability to certain gases, liquid media or some critical working environment are usually the basic requirements for MOF materials.
As generally acknowledged in material science, defects including surface, point, line or volume defects have remarkable influence on the physical and chemical properties of condensed matter. Since MOFs consist of regularly repeating crystal structures, their pores are expected to have precisely the same size, shape and functionality and this behaviour enables relationships between structures and properties to be established [133]. However, by introducing defects in MOFs, such internal orderliness is interrupted and thus considerably modify their behaviour for the targeted practices such as by resulting to (a) improved gas or liquid diffusion and mass transport caused by missing linker and metal vacancies [131], (b) enhanced catalytic performance caused by the open metal sites, and other impacts on magnetic, electronic and optical functionalities [134]. In fact, the presence and nature of structural defects has been found to shape the material's physical and chemical behaviour, and the correlation between the structural defects and MOF physical and chemical properties has been recognized [134]. A recent review by Taddei [135] have
reported that MOF defects have a significant influence on properties such as porosity, mechanical properties, thermal properties, as well as Lewis and Brønsted acidity. Similarly, DeStefano et al. [33] reported that it is possible to systematically control the density of defects in UiO-66 and three of its derivatives (UiO-66-X (X = NH₂, OH, or NO₂)). They suggested that their proposed strategy could be applied to incorporate temperature-sensitive functional groups in the MOFs and/or even lead to preparation of novel composite materials. In other words, there are necessities for introducing certain types of defects into MOF structures towards practices of interests. However, as discussed in Section 2, the concentration of defects needs to be balanced against application-oriented requirements, since sometimes the defective MOFs did not necessarily lead to better textural properties compared to the pristine samples [136–138].
## 4.1. Sorption and separation: defects tune pore sizes and binding energy
As a relatively new class of porous materials, the most obvious potential applications for MOF materials will be their use as sorbents to adsorb gases and/or separate liquid molecules. In the practices of hydrogen storage, as an example, the kinetic diameter of hydrogen molecule (2.89 Å) can be understood as the diameter of a pore needed to let hydrogen molecule pass [139]. Therefore, earlier findings [140,141] indicated that micropores with diameter just slightly over twice of that value (i.e. 6 Å) would have strengthened interactions between hydrogen molecules and pore walls, and some MOF materials have pore dimensions within this range, which makes them attractive. There are several studies that have concentrated on tuning the MOF pores to achieve desirable sizes for hydrogen storage [142,143] and some have even investigated the effect of defects for this case. For example, studies in our group
involved the correlation of structural defects in MOF materials such as UiO-66 and hydrogen sorption capacities [100]. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images in Fig. 24 allowed us to
conclude that addition of formic acid as a modulator not only could lead to creation of defects but also allowed the crystals to grow into much larger sizes with well-defined octahedral shapes.
The XRD, \(\mathrm{N}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\) sorption and pore size distribution shown in Fig. 25 indicated that when MOF growth time was longer than \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), the interaction of the modulator with reactants generated more defects. The excess defects exhibited adverse effects on the porosities of the MOF samples, as indicated by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\) sorption isotherms and pore size distribution curves.
Theoretically, the large dimensions of the secondary building unit (SBU) contribute to the pore size distributions greater than \(10\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) in many MOFs. In case smaller pore sizes are desired, the use of short linker can be a choice, for instance, synthesis of Zr-fumarate (fumarate acid as linker) MOF with smaller micropore diameters of \(5-7\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) sorption applications [142–144]. However, such strategy is not always guaranteed since the microporosity might not be accessible due to the too small unit cell formed but studies by Bueken et al. [145] created accessible microporosity by the incorporation of different modulators during the synthesis. Alternatively, the interpenetrating MOF structures could significantly decrease the pore sizes, for instance, the incorporation of a microporous MOF material into another mesoporous MOF material to optimize the pore geometry and surface structure has also shown promise to achieve higher gas uptake [146–148]. Technically, this can also be classified as one of the defect-introducing methods.
In addition, incorporating unsaturated metal centres in MOF structures can be used to achieve enhanced binding energies. Three strategies have been proposed by Dinca et al. [149] for incorporating unsaturated metal centres in MOF structures: (a) metal building units with coordinatively unsaturated centres through solvent removal, (b) incorporating coordinatively unsaturated metal sites within the organic linkers, and (c) impregnation of MOFs with metal ions. From literature, some other strategies were also reported to create defects in MOF structures such as modulator promoted defects [150–153], metal alkoxide functionalized linkers [154], linker fragmentation [155], ligand functionalization [156,157], sulphone-functionalization [158] and carboxylate-/iod o-functionalization [159,160].
Given the fact that the majority of the stable MOFs such as \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)}\) are microporous materials [24,161], for certain applications such as adsorbing dyes with large molecules, the pore sizes must be expanded [162]. This modification can be achieved by following some of the MOF defects' creation strategies [150–160]. These defects would facilitate the sorption of large target molecules by improving on the diffusion limitations. For example, Fan et al. [161] reported on the effect of using defective MOFs for rhodamine B (RhB) dye degradation and showed that defect-engineered sample processed improved photocatalytic degradation efficiency. In a
different study, Zhou et al. [131] also showed that a defective UiO-66 could be the reason for the enhanced dichloromethane adsorption capacity.
As shown in Fig. 26, the sorption experiments on two probe molecules: Safranine T (ST, about \(9.2 \times 11.4\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) in size) and crystal violet (CV, about \(12.3 \times 13.9\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) in size) revealed that the resulting MOF could selectively capture ST from mixture of ST and CV due to the size-exclusion effect, which indicated that the strategy was useful for the selective separation of large molecules with different sizes.
Huang et al. [163] adopted an in-situ self-assembly template strategy to fabricate various stable hierarchical-pore MOFs (H-MOFs), in which multi-scale soluble and/or acid-sensitive metal–organic assembly fragments formed as the templates during the reactions, and subsequently served as removable dynamic chemical templates. As shown in Fig. 27, the resulting H-MOFs have size-tunable mesopores in the range of \(38{-}123\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) for potential applications such as large molecule adsorption/desorption. Through such strategy, stable H-MOFs with tailorable pore sizes can be simply fabricated based on their stable parent microporous MOFs. However, the exact nature of the templates was not identified in this work.
Recently, Cliffe et al. [164] explored the chemistry from 3D fcu UiO-67(Zr) to form 2D hcp UiO-67(Zr) materials consisting of stacked 2D metal–organic sheets by using the modulator approach to control the ligand:metal ratio. The ligand-deficiency in the resulting hcp UiO-67(Zr) material was accommodated by the condensation of the hafnium oxide nodes to yield a double cluster \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{14}\), in an analogous manner to variable stoichiometry oxides.
As shown in Fig. 28, it is possible to use defect engineering to introduce 'weak links' into the envisioned MOF structures and create the corresponding 2D metal–organic nanosheets for potential applications such as large molecule adsorption. More interestingly,
the reversibility of the transformation offers the feasibility to create new families of 3D MOFs by conducting these re-assemblies with differently functionalized ligands, as a form of postsynthetic ligand exchange. Another example was demonstrated by Zhao et al. [165] for response to methanol vapour. The performance of sensors can be optimized by tuning defect densities of UiO-66(Zr) crystals and the higher recovery speed of the crystal-sensor was ascribed to the more missing linker defects. The oxide and/or water/hydroxide were hypothesized to compensate for the residual defects' sites in \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) clusters with missing linkers.
4.2. Adsorption heat pump: defects tune hydrophobic and hydrophilic properties
The main challenge of the research in this field is to develop a compact and efficient adsorption system. MOF materials such as CPO-27(Ni), Al-fumarate MOF and MIL-101(Cr) have been demonstrated to have high water adsorption capacities, which make them promising as alternative adsorbents for the conventional zeolites and silica gel towards heat transformation applications like thermally driven heat pumps or adsorption chillers [166–168]. The working principle is shown in Fig. 29 [169].
As a cooling application, the quantity of heat that can be extracted from the evaporator per adsorption cycle determines the adsorbent/adsorptive working pair that is chosen [170]. Therefore, MOF materials with high water load capacity and hydrothermal stability are desired. However, most of the water-stable MOFs have the hydrophobic nature. As discussed in Section 3, the linker defective sites in MOF structure would be easily occupied by water. It is promising to achieve higher water load capacity by creating missing linking defects in MOF structure. Thanks to the early studies, the presence of defects was found to increase the hydrophilicity of MOF materials, and the removal of hydrophobic \(\mathrm{bdc}^{2-}\) linker most likely plays the main role in increasing the affinity for water.
As a matter of fact, research has indicated that the water uptake is a direct function of the number of defects [171,172].
As a matter of fact, research has indicated that the water uptake is a direct function of the number of defects [171,172].
4.3. Catalysis: defects create Brønsted or Lewis acid sites
4.3. Catalysis: defects create Brønsted or Lewis acid sitesFrom literature, research has been focused on two routes to alter the catalytic behaviours of MOFs: (a) immobilization of catalytic species into MOF structures enables stabilization, easy separation and recyclability [173], and (b) creation of defect-based Brønsted or Lewis acid sites to achieve better catalytic performance [174–177]. The concept of 'nanoconfinement' has been introduced to promote certain reactions by employing MOFs as scaffolds [178–193]. For the process of nanoconfinement, the nanopores and ordered crystalline lattice are expected to provide an environment that is very controllable and intrinsically comprehensible. In this regard, few defects in the MOF structure would be required from the synthetic processes. Considering the scope of this review, this content will not be discussed here.In the field of MOF science, the presence of oxygen vacancy could be considered as a missing linker in the framework
In the field of MOF science, the presence of oxygen vacancy could be considered as a missing linker in the framework
(Fig. 30a). On a different note, the presence of the coordinative unsaturated atoms at the surface of an electron poor noble gold particle in the MOF structure results to the typical Lewis catalytic sites (Fig. 30b). Canivet et al. [194] have provided a comprehensive review depicting defective MOFs from a catalysis point of view. A study by Park et al. [195] indicated that defect sites with functionalized dangling carboxylates in MOF-5 facilitated the achievement of heterogeneous catalytic properties with highly dispersed Pd nanoparticles. Vermoortele et al. [196] proposed an increase in activity of MOF catalysts with coordinatively unsaturated sites by using functional linkers. A clear linear free-energy relationship between the electronic nature of the linker substituents and the reaction rate was found to exist in a carbonyl-ene reaction. Later, synthesis modulation was proposed by the same group as a means to increase the catalytic activity of MOFs. During their study, the combined use of trifluoroacetic acid and hydrochloric acid during the synthesis process and later thermal activation resulted in a highly active catalyst for several Lewis acid catalysed reactions [197]. Taylor et al. [198,199] found the positive role of the ordered defect
sub-lattice on the acidity of a sulphonated MOF, which consequently increased the proton conductivity.
As a summary from literature [194] (Fig. 31), several strategies have been employed to create defects in MOFs for better catalytic properties: (a) generation of linker vacancies during MOF synthe
sis; (b) linker substitution with lower coordination mode; (c) metal cation isomorphous substitution at cornerstone; and (d) development of micro-mesoporous hierarchical porosity.
## 4.4. Electrochemical applications: overall acidity
4.4. Electrochemical applications: overall acidityLigand defects present in MOFs have been demonstrated to have crucial effects on their proton conductivity and subsequently on their catalytic performance [204–207]. The common method for improving proton conductivity of MOFs is through increasing overall acidity, mainly by controlling the formation of ligand defects. In addition, metal ions and counter-ions could be grafted at defect sites in order to regulate the ionic conductivity, and the ensuing MOF materials could be potentially valuable for improving the operation of next-generation lithium batteries [208–210].
## 4.5. Bio-applications: defects tune surface functionality and pore-sizes
The utilization of MOFs as carriers of active biological agent provides more effective therapies by lowering the required amount of the active ingredients such as pharmaceutical ingredient (API) for cancer treatments. Teplensky et al. [211] developed a mild temperature treatment protocol to cause partial pore collapse of MOFs NU-1000 and NU-901, thus delaying the release of a model compound calcein as well as the anticancer therapeutic \(\pmb{\{x\}}\) -Cyano-4- hydroxycinnamic acid ( \(\pmb{\{x\}}\) -CHC). Through this work, they showed the potential of MOF engineering capable of providing long-term controlled release of envisioned drugs.
4.5. Bio-applications: defects tune surface functionality and pore-sizesThe utilization of MOFs as carriers of active biological agent provides more effective therapies by lowering the required amount of the active ingredients such as pharmaceutical ingredient (API) for cancer treatments. Teplensky et al. [211] developed a mild temperature treatment protocol to cause partial pore collapse of MOFs NU-1000 and NU-901, thus delaying the release of a model compound calcein as well as the anticancer therapeutic \(\pmb{\{x\}}\) -Cyano-4- hydroxycinnamic acid ( \(\pmb{\{x\}}\) -CHC). Through this work, they showed the potential of MOF engineering capable of providing long-term controlled release of envisioned drugs.As shown in Fig. 32, the authors evaluated the drug efficacy by means of a metabolic activity assay, where a lower value of cell viability implies a higher efficacy of the drug. They measured the viability of cells in the presence of \(\pmb{\{x\}}\) -CHC alone as a control (free drug), as well as with \(\pmb{\{x\}}\) -CHC loaded into both crystalline and temperature-treated MOF, for quantification of the effectiveness of these MOFs to deliver the drug to cells. The anticancer drug exhibited better efficacy when loaded into the temperature-treated MOF (defective MOF). Analogous to improvements created by MOF defects for other applications, the control of defects can also be expected to lead to surface functionality and modifications in pore-sizes which would enhance the applicability of MOFs as drug carriers.
## 4.6. Smart applications: defects alter the electronic band structure
4.6. Smart applications: defects alter the electronic band structureAs mentioned in the introductory section, defects in MOFs could alter their physical properties compared to their native counterparts. Defects in MOF materials have recently been reported to alter their electronic band structure [134]. For example, a value of \(2.87\,\mathrm{eV}\) was obtained for the hydrated parent HKUST-1, while larger band gaps of \(3.31{-}3.87\,\mathrm{eV}\) were obtained in truncated mixed linker (TML)-modified HKUST-1 [212]. This enhancement has been ascribed to the doped amount of linker fragments and also the simultaneous partial reduction of \(\mathrm{{Cu}}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{{Cu^{2+}}}\) . Structural defects have also been noted to affect the thermal conductivity of MOFs by altering phonon transport. As revealed from early work [213,214], there is evidence of dependence of the thermal conductivity of MOF-5 on the mode of phonon scattering that is induced by the presence of lattice defects. The purposeful introduction of metal ion defects has also been reported to open a potential path for modifying the magnetic properties of MOFs. For example, ferromagnetic coupling between \(\mathrm{{Cu}}^{2+}\) paddlewheels was observed in HKUST-1 [215], Shen et al. [44] also reported the introduction of ferromagnetism by vacancy defects. Their studies indicated the dependence of the observed ferromagnetism on point defects \(\mathrm{{[Cu}}^{2+}\) vacancies).
## 4.7. Towards other-oriented applications: defects' healing
From the discussion above, the control of defects can be realized towards practices of interest. Apart from the compromised
mechanical stability caused by the disorders in the MOF structures [216,217], there is also need for a balance between performance and concentration of defects contained in the MOF structure. The detailed information of controlling formation and concentration of defects would aid the envisioned practices in a positive way. Sholl et al. [28] proposed four valuable points concerning 'defects in MOFs: challenge or opportunity?': "(a) the likely ubiquitous nature of defects in MOFs implies that efforts to systematically understand them will yield considerable benefits in controlling material properties; (b) in general, guiding principles for the nature and concentration of defect sites and their relationship to deviations in performance from ideal MOF crystals have not yet been develop; (c) Extended defects of various kinds are likely to be common in MOFs and also the equally important observation that whether these defects will have an important influence on the performance of the crystal is dependent on the application that is envisioned for the materials, and (d) the external surfaces of MOFs are likely to be a rich source of deviations from ideal crystal structures." From the elucidations, the synthetic origin of these defects is not always beneficial for some particular applications. Generally, the concentration of missing linker can be reduced by increasing the reaction temperature and starting linker/metal ratio [218]. This is understandable based on the principle of modulation synthesis since linker molecules compete with the modulator molecules in the reaction process. Noting that missing linkers are the underlying cause of most of the drawbacks towards some targeted applications, the capability towards "defect-free" product becomes essential. Lee et al. [219] proved the possibility of 'defect-healing' by the introduction of free ligands in a solvent-assisted approach to restore pristine MUF-32. Han et al. [220] proposed a regeneration protocol by firstly dissolving deteriorated MOFs in strong acid, and then adjusting the pH of the solution via either in-situ base formation or ex-situ base addition, thereby regenerating pristine MOFs in high yields. The processes are shown in Fig. 33.
Lillerud group [91] also proposed that an increase in the synthesis temperature and/or BDC:Zr ratio shifts the solution equilibrium in favour of BDC-Zr bonds, thereby helping the "ironing out" of linker deficiencies. The suggested strategy eventually yields "ideal" UiO-66 with unmatched thermal stability when the synthesis was conducted with a 2:1 BDC:Zr ratio at \(220\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . As a relatively new class of highly porous materials, from literature, MOFs have
been demonstrated in various applications and control of defects can be implemented accordingly.
## 5. Some controversies and knowledge gaps on MOF defects
In as much as the study of MOF defects is a hot topic with growing interest, there are still some knowledge gaps and controversies when it comes to the understanding of nature and type of MOF defect that still need to be addressed in order to bring out more clarity on the subject. Some of the main controversial issues were recently summarized by Taddei [135] and are as follows; the debate on the dualism of missing-linker versus missing-cluster and also the ambiguity on the nature of the compensating species. In the missing linker/missing cluster controversy, there is still uncertainty as to whether both types of defects can be exclusively distinguished from each other owing to the complex nature of their coexistence. On the other hand, there is still some ongoing debate about the type and nature of the species that fills the coordination vacancies at defective sites. These substituting species are meant to ensure electro neutrality of the MOF structure.
In addition to the defects controversies, there is also a need for more systematic studies on understanding the effects of using functionalized linkers and how it influences the MOF defects. Further investigations are recommended to enable the understanding of the role of defects during the post-synthesis modifications of MOFs. Some of the stated controversies and knowledge gaps in the study of MOF defects could be solved by insights that could be brought in by carrying out more studies on understanding the MOFs crystallization mechanism, which is still at its infancy when compared to advances in zeolite chemistry. Even though most MOF defects' studies are conducted on laboratory scale environments, there is also need to investigate methods for producing reproducible and large-scale quantities of MOFs with controllable defects attributes. In a recent study, Zhao et al. [221] reported an acid/base co-modulation strategy to the large-scale synthesis of monodisperse UiO-66 crystals and tuned the formation of missing linker defects. Another area that could benefit from more research is on application of MOF defect engineering to create MOF nanosheets from 3D MOFs [166]. Since it is often assumed that the interactions between modulators and metal centres do not happen when the metal centre has low coordination numbers [28], this stays as a controversy that will need further investigation to improve the understanding of the very nature of defects.
## 6. Conclusion and outlook
In recent years, research into metal–organic frameworks' (MOFs) materials has transitioned from laboratories to application-oriented practices. Starting from the early excitement on MOF materials as microporous materials for storage of gases (such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)), with such enthusiasm arising from MOFs typical textural properties such as high surface areas and pore volumes, which are important parameters for adsorption of gases. However, the poor moisture-resistance of certain MOFs such as MOF-5 has hindered their further employment on a wider scale. The later discovered MOFs such as UiO-66 (Zr) and HKUST-1 (Cu) with good chemical and thermal stabilities, overcome this barrier. The synthesis of MIL-101(Cr) using water as a solvent, eliminating the use of environmentally unfriendly organic solvents enables the production of MOF materials at a reasonable cost. Meanwhile, the newly-discovered properties of MOF materials have extended their potential range of applications far beyond their use as traditional microporous materials. The presence of structural defects in MOFs was recognized since the first indication of poor thermal stability from what is considered as 'defect-free' MOF structure. As
generally accepted in material science, structural defects would have impact on the physical properties of condensed matter. From the recent research, the defects in MOF structures have been proved to alter their behaviour for the envisioned applications such as by leading to (a) improved gas diffusion and mass transport caused by missing linker and metal vacancies, (b) enhanced catalytic performance caused by the open metal sites, and other impacts on electronic, magnetic and optical functionalities. In fact, the presence and nature of structural defects has been shown to shape the physical and chemical behaviour of the material, and the relationship between structural defects and MOF physical and chemical properties has been acknowledged. Therefore, it is important to introduce certain defects into MOF structures towards practices of interests, but the concentration of defects must be tuned to application-oriented requirements. Table 3 summarizes the control of structural defects in MOFs towards practices of interests.
